# Type-inference soundness check over one corpus, in three steps. The
# interpreter runs the corpus with a recorder installed and writes what every
# register actually held (ctcompile-test-type-oracle); the compiler imports the
# same corpus, runs the inference, and writes what it claims every register
# will hold (ctcompile-test-type-claims); tools/check/type-oracle.py compares
# the two and requires zero soundness violations. Precision is reported but
# never required: a wrong claim is a defect, a weak one is only a backlog.
# `observed` and `beat-boxed` must both be positive, so an inference that falls
# back to `boxed` everywhere (sound, but useless) cannot pass vacuously. A value
# in a live block the solver never visited is an analysis gap the emitter
# hides behind `boxed`, so that count must be zero. The oracle's own self-test
# runs first.
#
# RUN: ctcompile-test-type-oracle
# The bundle and the driver go into one file: both sides compile one file into
# one program, and its source hash is what joins a claim to an observation.
# RUN: cat %{monorepo}/ctbrowser/vendor/bootstrap/bootstrap.bundle.js > %t.js && echo >> %t.js && cat %S/../bootstrap-driver.js >> %t.js
# RUN: ctcompile-test-type-oracle --script %t.js --out %t.rec
# RUN: ctcompile-test-type-claims --script %t.js --out %t.claims 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=CLAIMS
# CLAIMS: {{[^0-9]}}0 unvisited live values boxed
# RUN: python3 %{monorepo}/tools/check/type-oracle.py --recording %t.rec --claims %t.claims --name bootstrap --max-report 0 --expect-violations 0 | FileCheck %s
# CHECK: observed registers {{[1-9][0-9]*}}
# CHECK: SOUNDNESS violations 0{{[^0-9]}}
# CHECK: PRECISION beat-boxed {{[1-9][0-9]*}}/{{[0-9]+}} = {{[0-9.]+}}%
